<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim Zaki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim Zaki was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar and commander who took up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> on the western frontier of the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>. In 1808 he joined other Fulani leaders in capturing the Bornu capital, Birni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngazargamu">Ngazargamu</a>. He founded the Katagum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-zaki.jpg" alt="Ibrahim Zaki" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The President's Office, Maldives · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibrahim_Hussain_Zaki_special_envoy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
